  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "Agentic LLMs are moving from text generation to tool-invoking analysis, now evaluated on clinical genomics, scRNA-seq and spatial omics.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is coupling LLMs to executable tools, curated skills, persistent memory, or trained predictors so that outputs are grounded and checkable. ClinGeX composes 105 executable skills for clinical genomic analysis, reporting 71.7% macro-averaged concordance with expert classifications. SMART is a multi-agent framework testing whether scRNA-seq cancer findings transfer across six cancer types across 121 studies. LabWeaver adds laboratory-tier memory and skills to agentic data analysis, and SPaRTA provides a text-to-spatial-SQL interface for spatial-omics data with a 7,500-pair benchmark. SurvXAI pairs Random Survival Forest predictions and SurvSHAP attributions with locally run LLMs that generate narrative explanations. The Rosalind benchmark, evaluating GPT-5.2 and Claude Sonnet 4.6 on 104 multi-step bioinformatics questions against expert and 68,760 human answers, supplies a reference point. The novelty lies in agents that invoke domain tools with traceable evidence and in explanation layers over interpretable survival models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-04"
}
